<Docs title="Security model & current limitations">
  <div class="doc-title">
    <span class="section-kicker">SECURITY / RESPONSIBILITIES</span>
    <h1>Define what you trust.</h1>
    <p>AgentGuard is an authorization component. Its guarantees depend on the enforcement path, trusted request facts, and operational configuration.</p>
  </div>
  <h2>What the engine enforces</h2>
  <p>Cedar evaluates supplied principal, action, resource, context, and entities against the loaded policy set. No matching permit means deny; an applicable forbid overrides permits. The decision controls execution only when the adapter honors it.</p>
  <h2>What it does not protect</h2>
  <p>Authorization does not detect every prompt injection, prove that model output is correct, patch tool vulnerabilities, or stop an unguarded direct API call. Broadly permitted actions can still be harmful. An authorization allow is not proof of successful or safe tool execution.</p>
  <h2>Trusted inputs and identity</h2>
  <p>Standalone PDP API keys require an explicit scope and are bound to one User or Agent subject. Evaluation requests with another subject are rejected. Optional key tenant metadata is trusted for audit and request tracking but is not itself a Cedar policy attribute; tenant isolation must be encoded through trusted entity data and policy inputs. Never let a model or untrusted caller provision its own bound key.</p>
  <p>MFA, environment, resource ownership, and group membership must come from trusted systems, not model-supplied arguments. See the <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/docs/identity.md">identity guide</a> for key provisioning and migration.</p>
  <h2>Enforcement and failures</h2>
  <p>Intercept every relevant tool path, including retries and delegated calls. Execute only on explicit allow and successful completion of the decision interface. Deny, timeout, invalid response, or audit failure must not become implicit permission. The Strands guard demonstrates fail-closed HTTP handling.</p>
  <p>The CLI writes its decision to stdout before completing audit persistence. Wait for a successful exit status as well as checking the effect. The embedded Authorizer does not write audit automatically.</p>
  <h2>Current operating limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Generated init policies are broad development grants; replace both before deployment.</li>
    <li>Server file watching and SIGHUP replace the standalone policy snapshot only after complete validation; malformed edits retain the last-known-good snapshot.</li>
    <li>Standalone decision caching is enabled by default and can be tuned with the documented cache variables; OTLP export remains an embedding-library capability.</li>
    <li>JWT, DPoP, and SPIFFE are library capabilities, not standalone server auth modes.</li>
    <li>The console simulator uses a privileged <code>authorize:any</code> service key to evaluate selected subjects; protect it server-side and restrict console users through OIDC/RBAC.</li>
    <li>Delegation signature verification does not enforce scope or prove parent authority. Applications must implement those checks and any revocation service.</li>
    <li>gRPC is plaintext and restricted to loopback.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Audit trust</h2>
  <p>Enable HMAC chaining with a secret. Protect the key and original records; retain trusted independent head checkpoints. A secret holder can forge logs, and chain verification without a checkpoint cannot establish that all original records remain.</p>
  <h2>Production readiness</h2>
  <p>This source contains authorization, audit, and identity components with a documented Docker/Kubernetes reference deployment. Production support is scoped to that path and its stated limitations; validate your precise interface, threat model, failure behavior, and policy lifecycle before rollout. Stable API/LTS guarantees remain outside the current compatibility promise.</p>
  <h2>Report a vulnerability privately</h2>
  <p>Follow <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/SECURITY.md">SECURITY.md</a> and GitHub private vulnerability reporting. Do not open a public issue containing exploit details. Only the latest released version is listed as receiving security fixes.</p>
</Docs>
